# Installed as /etc/default/piboy-batd. Read by piboy-batd.service.
#
# Normal running needs nothing here. The gauge loads its calibration from
# /var/lib/piboy/battery-cal.json if present, and falls back to the generic
# constants compiled into the daemon otherwise - it says which, on startup:
#
#     journalctl -u piboy-batd -n 5
#
# TO CALIBRATE THIS PACK
#
# 1. Charge to full, then uncomment the line below and restart:
#
#        sudo systemctl restart piboy-batd
#
# 2. Use the handheld normally until it shuts itself down. The fit needs the
#    load to VARY - menu idling and actual play both - because it recovers pack
#    resistance from how voltage moves against current within a slice of charge.
#    A session held at one constant load cannot be fitted and will be refused.
#
# 3. Charge back up, boot, and fit:
#
#        sudo /home/cytzenx/piboy-src/fit-battery-curve.py /var/log/piboy-batt.csv
#
#    That writes /var/lib/piboy/battery-cal.json. Check it before trusting it:
#
#        piboy-batd --replay /var/log/piboy-batt.csv
#
# 4. Empty BATD_ARGS again and restart. The daemon picks up the calibration on
#    its next start; no file needs editing by hand.
#
# Set, rather than commented out: systemd logs a warning on every start for a
# referenced-but-unset variable, and that noise outlives the one time it is useful.
BATD_ARGS=
# To capture, swap the line above for:
#BATD_ARGS=--log /var/log/piboy-batt.csv
